# Patches are byte-exact input to `git apply`, not text to be normalised. advancedfx's
# tree mixes LF and CRLF, so a hunk's line endings have to survive a checkout on any
# machine -- `core.autocrlf` rewriting them turns a clean apply into a rejected hunk, and
# the error it produces says nothing about line endings.
*.patch -text
*.diff -text

# Windows shells read these; keep CRLF so they behave the same when checked out anywhere.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# CS2 reads these; it is not fussy, but they are Windows files.
*.cfg text eol=crlf

# Our C++ is copied into the advancedfx tree, which is LF for these files.
*.cpp text eol=lf
*.h   text eol=lf

*.md  text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
